{"name":"Silver Strand","hex":"#b8c7ce","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A lustrous, white, metallic element, atomic number 47, atomic weight 107.87, symbol Ag.","Coins made from silver or any similar white metal."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made from silver.","Made from another white metal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver"},{"term":"strand","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The shore or beach of the sea or ocean.","The shore or beach of a lake or river."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To run aground; to beach.","To run aground."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/strand"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silver Strand (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Silver Strand is a 1995 action/drama/romance film starring Nicollette Sheridan, Gil Bellows, Jennifer O'Neill, Jay O. Sanders, Tony Plana and Wolfgang Bodison. It was directed by George Miller and written by Douglas Day Stewart. The story follows Class 195 through United States Navy SEAL selection and training known as Basic Underwater Demolition/SEAL (BUD/S). Brian Del Piso falls for the wife of his commanding officer, Lucas Hughes. Michelle Hughes was a Navy brat and is now Lucas wife."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Strand_(film)"},{"title":"Silver Strand Beach","blocks":[{"text":"Silver Strand Beach is an unincorporated beach neighborhood in Ventura County, California, United States. Lying between the mouth of Channel Islands Harbor on the north and the ocean entrance to the Port of Hueneme on the south, the eastern side of the beach community lies along the boundary of the Naval Construction Battalion Center Port Hueneme. The harbors and the Naval base almost completely surround Silver Strand, keeping the area relatively separate from the city of Oxnard and making it accessible by only one main road, Victoria Avenue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Silver_Strand_State_Beach_San_Diego%2C_California_Aerial_Photography.jpg/330px-Silver_Strand_State_Beach_San_Diego%2C_California_Aerial_Photography.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Strand_Beach"},{"title":"Silver Strand (San Diego County)","blocks":[{"text":"Silver Strand, or simply The Strand, is a low, narrow, sandy isthmus or a human-constructed tombolo 7 miles (11 km) long in San Diego County, California, partially within Silver Strand State Beach. It connects Coronado with Imperial Beach. Together with the Point Loma peninsula it shelters and defines San Diego Bay."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Silver_Strand_sign.jpg/330px-Silver_Strand_sign.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Strand_(San_Diego_County)"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Silver-Strand-Beach-California.jpg/500px-Silver-Strand-Beach-California.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Silver-Strand-Beach-California.jpg/1280px-Silver-Strand-Beach-California.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Silver-Strand-Beach-California.jpg/500px-Silver-Strand-Beach-California.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Silver Strand Beach, January 2015","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASilver-Strand-Beach-California.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/3e/Mural_Wall_SSB.jpg/330px-Mural_Wall_SSB.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/3e/Mural_Wall_SSB.jpg/330px-Mural_Wall_SSB.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Mural Wall at The Ship parking lot, Silverstrand Beach.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMural_Wall_SSB.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Silver_Strand_State_Beach_sign.jpg/500px-Silver_Strand_State_Beach_sign.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Silver_Strand_State_Beach_sign.jpg/1280px-Silver_Strand_State_Beach_sign.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Silver_Strand_State_Beach_sign.jpg/500px-Silver_Strand_State_Beach_sign.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Northern extreme of the east side of Silver Strand State Beach","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASilver_Strand_State_Beach_sign.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg/500px-1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia